# Who is asked to review what.
#
# The value of this file is that it removes a decision from the contributor: a pull
# request to a repository with one maintainer otherwise sits until someone notices it, and
# "waiting to be noticed" is indistinguishable from "rejected" from the outside.
#
# The rules are ordered least specific first, because the last matching pattern wins, so
# the most specific rule for a path is the one stated last.
#
# The specification is a separate repository and is not covered here; a change that moves
# a normative term belongs in `amasario-provenance-spec` and is reviewed there. See
# `CONTRIBUTING.md`.

# Everything, unless something below is more specific.
*               @DevDDSmart

# The CI definition and the dependency policy are the gates the rest of the repository is
# judged by. A pull request that loosens a gate while claiming to fix a test is the one
# case where the change and its justification can disagree without anyone noticing, so
# these are called out explicitly.
/.github/       @DevDDSmart
/deny.toml      @DevDDSmart
/clippy.toml    @DevDDSmart

# The security policy states what the engine refuses to claim, and the profile states the
# boundaries an analysis runs under. Both are read by people making deployment decisions,
# so a change to either is a change to the tool's contract rather than to its behaviour.
/SECURITY.md               @DevDDSmart
/profiles/                 @DevDDSmart

# Committed fixtures are compared byte for byte against documents the engine rebuilds, and
# the recorded responses under `fixtures/` are transcriptions of what a network endpoint
# actually said. A hand-edited one changes what the suites assert without changing any
# test, which is the quietest way for this repository to start verifying the wrong thing.
/fixtures/      @DevDDSmart
